d b+c+d=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1980_AMC_12_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h204","cnm_168","cnm_096"],"id":"locate-odd-number-blocks","label":"Locate the blocks using square numbers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"locate-odd-number-blocks":["math:floor-ceiling","cnm_h199"]},"nodeIds":["math:floor-ceiling","cnm_h199","cnm_h204","cnm_168","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 6r-1","reason":"For r≥2, a_r=S_r-S_(r-1)=2+3(r^2-(r-1)^2)=6r-1. The formula also gives a_1=5=S_1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p035","cnm_029","cnm_034"],"id":"first-term-and-difference","label":"Find the first term and common difference","methodDirectNodeIds":{"first-term-and-difference":["cnm_h199","cnm_h201","cnm_h202"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h199","cnm_h201","cnm_h202","cnm_p035","cnm_029","cnm_034"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p035","cnm_029","cnm_034","cnm_035"],"id":"subtract-consecutive-sums","label":"Subtract consecutive partial sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtract-consecutive-sums":["cnm_h199","cnm_036","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h199","cnm_036","cnm_148","cnm_p035","cnm_029","cnm_034","cnm_035"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (2018)","reason":"Since 2017=1+2·1008, the odd branch applies 1008 times. Thus f(2017)=f(1)+2·1008=2+2016=2018.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12A_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"verify-recursive-formula","label":"Find and verify a formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"verify-recursive-formula":["cnm_h200","cnm_h199"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h200","cnm_h199"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, d and n","reason":"Using S_j=ja+j(j-1)d/2, S_(3n)-S_(2n)-S_n=2n²d. The first term a cancels, while d and n remain.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q36","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h201","cnm_h199"],"id":"solution:pair-shifted-terms:b9798fa149ef","label":"Pair terms with equally spaced indices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pair-shifted-terms":["cnm_h202"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h202","cnm_h201","cnm_h199"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"4","reason":"a_{n+1}-a_n=[4(n+1)-1]-(4n-1)=4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h205_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h199","cnm_036"],"id":"m2","label":"Use the common-difference definition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m2":["cnm_h201"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h201","cnm_h199","cnm_036"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: (n^2+3n)/4","reason":"The common difference is 1/2, so x_n=(n+1)/2. The sum is n[1+(n+1)/2]/2=(n^2+3n)/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h201","cnm_h199"],"id":"pair-the-sum-with-its-reversal","label":"Pair terms from opposite ends","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pair-the-sum-with-its-reversal":["cnm_h202","cnm_h204"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h202","cnm_h204","cnm_h201","cnm_h199"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 4015","reason":"The sum of the first n terms is S_n=n times n=n^2. Therefore a_n=S_n-S_(n-1)=n^2-(n-1)^2=2n-1, giving a_2008=4015.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12B_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h197","cnm_147"],"id":"2008_AMC_12B_Q12_m1","label":"Subtract consecutive totals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"2008_AMC_12B_Q12_m1":["cnm_p129","cnm_h199"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p129","cnm_h199","cnm_h197","cnm_147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 17","reason":"If consecutive axis coordinates are u and v, the apex condition gives 3(v-u)^2 = 2(u+v). Starting at 0 and excluding repeated points yields A_n = (n(n+1)/3,0). Thus A_0A_16 = 272/3 < 100, whereas A_0A_17 = 102, so the least index is 17.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"endpoint-quadratic-recurrence","label":"Quadratic recurrence for endpoints","methodDirectNodeIds":{"endpoint-quadratic-recurrence":["cnm_222","cnm_h199"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_222","cnm_h199"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 8078","reason":"Let b_n = 1/a_n. Then b_n = 2b_(n-1) - b_(n-2), with b_1 = 1 and b_2 = 7/3. Hence b_n = (4n-1)/3 and a_2019 = 3/8075. Since 8075 is not divisible by 3, p+q = 3+8075 = 8078.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12A_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"explicit-term-induction","label":"Verify an explicit term by induction","methodDirectNodeIds":{"explicit-term-induction":["cnm_h199","cnm_h214"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h199","cnm_h214"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1014","reason":"For k≥3 the mean is 2028-k, so S_k=k(2028-k) and x_k=S_k-S_{k-1}=2029-2k for k≥4. Positivity gives n≤1014. The initial terms 2026, 2025, 2024 followed by this tail attain n=1014, with final term 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prefix-sum-differences","label":"Subtract consecutive totals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prefix-sum-differences":["cnm_211","cnm_h199","cnm_097","math:extremal-principle"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_211","cnm_h199","cnm_097","math:extremal-principle"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 17","reason":"The exponents satisfy b_0=0, b_1=1 and b_n=b_(n−1)+2b_(n−2), giving b_n=(2^n−(−1)^n)/3. Their sum is S_k=(2^(k+2)−3−(−1)^k)/6. The order of 2 modulo 19 is 18, so 19 divides S_k first at odd k=17 or even k=18. Therefore the smallest is 17; indeed S_17=87381=19·4599, making the product 2^4599.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"closed-form-power-cycle","label":"Closed form and power cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"closed-form-power-cycle":["cnm_h199","cnm_h210","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h199","cnm_h210","math:modular-powers"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 338551","reason":"The recurrence becomes n a_n=(n-1)a_(n-1)+2n-1, giving a_n=n+1/n. Hence the sum is 338350+200+Σ(1/n²). This last sum is greater than 1 and less than 1+Σ from n=2 to 100 of 1/[n(n-1)]=1.99. The floor is therefore 338551.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"weighted-recurrence-telescoping","label":"Telescope the weighted recurrence","methodDirectNodeIds":{"weighted-recurrence-telescoping":["cnm_h199","math:telescoping","cnm_h204"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h199","math:telescoping","cnm_h204"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"inductive-formula-verification","label":"Verify the formula by induction","methodDirectNodeIds":{"inductive-formula-verification":["cnm_h199","cnm_h214","math:telescoping"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h199","cnm_h214","math:telescoping"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1014","reason":"For k≥3, the prefix average is 2028-k, so S_k=k(2028-k). Hence x_k=S_k-S_(k-1)=2029-2k for k≥4. Positivity gives n≤1014, with x_1014=1. The initial terms 2026,2025,2024, followed by 2021,2019,...,1, satisfy strict decrease and every required average, attaining n=1014.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_036"],"id":"prefix-sum-differences","label":"Subtract consecutive prefix totals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prefix-sum-differences":["cnm_211","cnm_h199","cnm_097","math:extremal-principle"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h199","cnm_036","cnm_211","cnm_097","math:extremal-principle"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Root reviewed the displayed task, definedness and stated conventional interpretation.","derivedAnswer":"D; the first zero difference is order 4","reason":"Directly expand and subtract: Delta u=3n^2+3n+2; Delta^2 u=6n+6; Delta^3 u=6; Delta^4 u=0. The third is never zero. The operator is fully defined by the question, so this calculation does not need a general degree-drop theorem.","recordedAnswer":"D","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h199","cnm_034","cnm_035","cnm_145"],"id":"successive-symbolic-differences","label":"Apply the difference rule repeatedly","methodDirectNodeIds":{"successive-symbolic-differences":["cnm_036","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_036","cnm_146","cnm_h199","cnm_034","cnm_035","cnm_145"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: √2−1","reason":"Each averaging step multiplies the leading term of the geometric progression by (1+x)/2. Therefore ((1+x)/2)¹⁰⁰=2⁻⁵⁰. Since x>0, taking positive roots gives 1+x=√2 and x=√2−1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h040","cnm_h197","cnm_h199"],"id":"preserve-geometric-pattern","label":"Track the first term through averaging","methodDirectNodeIds":{"preserve-geometric-pattern":["cnm_h200","cnm_h207","cnm_h208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h200","cnm_h207","cnm_h208","cnm_h040","cnm_h197","cnm_h199"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 18","reason":"The first relation gives a₇=17/3, and the eleven-term sum gives a₉=77/11=7. Thus d=(7-17/3)/2=2/3. Solving 13=17/3+(k-7)(2/3) gives k=18.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1993_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h201","cnm_h199","cnm_040"],"id":"solution:midpoint-term-symmetry:d101d6030036","label":"Method 1: Pair terms around the middle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-term-symmetry":["cnm_h203","cnm_h202","cnm_h204","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h203","cnm_h202","cnm_h204","cnm_042","cnm_h201","cnm_h199","cnm_040"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h201","cnm_h199","cnm_040","cnm_088"],"id":"solution:general-term-linear-system:d101d6030036","label":"Method 2: Solve for the first term and common difference","methodDirectNodeIds":{"general-term-linear-system":["cnm_h202","cnm_h204","cnm_090","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h202","cnm_h204","cnm_090","cnm_042","cnm_h201","cnm_h199","cnm_040","cnm_088"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 8","reason":"Put s=sin(x), c=cos(x), and r=c/s. Geometricity gives c^3=s^2, so c^2(1+c)=1. Also a4=1 and r^2=1/c, hence a8=r^4=1/c^2=1+c. These relations force 0<c<1 and |r|>1, so the index is unique.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h197","cnm_h199","cnm_h060","cnm_h069"],"id":"fourth-term-and-ratio","label":"Start from the fourth term","methodDirectNodeIds":{"fourth-term-and-ratio":["cnm_h207","cnm_h062","cnm_h208"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_h062","cnm_h208","cnm_h197","cnm_h199","cnm_h060","cnm_h069"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (7; N=16)","reason":"Bernardo's kth output is 2^k(N+50)-100. He wins exactly when this lies in [950,1000), so 1050 <= 2^k(N+50) < 1100. For N>=0, only k=1,2,3,4 can work; their smallest admissible integers are 475,213,82,16. At N=16, Bernardo produces 956 and Silvia next produces 1006. Thus the digit sum is 1+6=7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_097","cnm_h197","cnm_h199","cnm_h207"],"id":"shift-to-doubling","label":"Method 2: Turn the output recurrence into repeated doubling","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shift-to-doubling":["cnm_h200","cnm_h208","cnm_h020","cnm_099"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h200","cnm_h208","cnm_h020","cnm_099","cnm_097","cnm_h197","cnm_h199","cnm_h207"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (1/4)","reason":"Let t_i=DQ_i. Geometry gives t_1=1/2 and t_(i+1)=t_i/(1+t_i), hence t_i=1/(i+1). Each area is t_i t_(i+1)/2=(t_i-t_(i+1))/2. The infinite sum is therefore t_1/2=1/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_268","cnm_p077"],"id":"nested-area-differences","label":"Subtract nested triangle areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"nested-area-differences":["math:telescoping","cnm_p148","cnm_271","cnm_27